\begin{equation}
  S_t = \rho(\mathbf{Z}) = \exp\!\left(-\sum_i p_i \log p_i\right)
\end{equation}
The effective rank $S_t$ measures the diversity of learned representations: collapse concentrates the singular spectrum, causing $S_t$ to decrease.
This signal is cheap to compute (one forward pass, no gradient) and prior work shows that calibration degradation (ECE) precedes accuracy loss by 1--2 iterations~\cite{calibration2025}; whether spectral signals exhibit similar or earlier lead time is an empirical question we investigate.

\subsection{Proportional Controller with Budget Constraint}

We smooth the raw signal with an exponential moving average:
\begin{equation}
  \tilde{S}_t = \alpha \cdot S_t + (1 - \alpha) \cdot \tilde{S}_{t-1}, \quad \alpha = 0.5
\end{equation}
and define the normalized decline rate:
\begin{equation}
  \Delta_t = \frac{\tilde{S}_{t-1} - \tilde{S}_t}{|\tilde{S}_0|}
\end{equation}

