\begin{equation}
  r_{t+1} = \text{clip}\!\left(r_{t+1}^{\text{raw}},\; r_{\min},\; \min\!\left(r_{\max},\; B_{\text{rem}} - (T - t - 1) \cdot r_{\min}\right)\right)
\end{equation}
guaranteeing that every future generation receives at least $r_{\min}$.
The first generation uses $r_1 = r_{\text{base}}$ (no prior signal).

Controller hyperparameters: threshold $\tau$, gain $k$, EMA coefficient $\alpha$, bounds $r_{\min}$ and $r_{\max}$.

\subsection{Experimental Design}

We compare three conditions sharing an identical total real-data budget $B$:
\begin{enumerate}[nosep]
  \item \textbf{Pure synthetic} ($r_t = 0\;\forall t$): collapse reference establishing degradation ceiling.
  \item \textbf{Fixed uniform} ($r_t = r_{\text{base}}\;\forall t$): static baseline representing naive budget-spreading.
  \item \textbf{Spectral-guided adaptive} (ours): closed-loop allocation via the controller above.
\end{enumerate}

